# 0006. Resource limits: where each one is enforced

- Status: Accepted
- Date: 2026-11-11

Every configured limit is checked at the one place that already has the information, so
memory stays bounded without extra round-trips.

## Technical details

**Problem.** Memory must stay bounded and clients must not exhaust the broker (SPEC §7, §10).
Stage 1 made every limit a setting (§14.4).

**Where each limit lives.**

| Limit (default) | Enforced in | How | On violation |
|---|---|---|---|
| `max_connections` (1024) | accept loop | `Semaphore::try_acquire_owned`; the permit moves into the connection task | `ERROR TOO_MANY_CONNECTIONS`, close |
| `max_queues` (1024) | registry (`src/broker.rs`) | count under the existing std mutex, before spawning | `TOO_MANY_QUEUES` |
| `max_queue_len` (10 000) | queue actor | `ready.len()` on publish | `QUEUE_FULL` |
| `max_total_bytes` (256 MiB) | `Broker::publish` | shared `AtomicUsize`, reserve with a compare-and-swap loop before sending to the actor; released on ACK, expiry, or a rejected publish | `QUEUE_FULL` |
| `max_subscriptions` (16) | connection | count of its subscriptions | `TOO_MANY_SUBSCRIPTIONS` |
| `max_message_size`, frame, queue name | connection | `read_frame` / `Request::validate` ([0002](0002-wire-protocol-ndjson.adr)) | as in PROTOCOL.md |

- Every channel is bounded.
- The `(proposed)` note on `max_total_bytes` in SPEC §14.4 is removed in this PR.

**Consequences.**
- Worst-case memory ≈ `max_total_bytes` + per-message overhead + channel buffers. (Revised by
  [0014](0014-per-message-overhead.adr): the overhead now counts toward `max_total_bytes`.)
- Queues are never deleted in v1, so `max_queues` slots are used up for the server's
  lifetime.

## Technologies and alternatives
- Involved: tokio `Semaphore`, std `Mutex`, `AtomicUsize`.
- Alternatives:
  - tokio `Mutex` for the registry: only needed when held across `.await`; it isn't.
  - Per-queue byte limits only: bounded per queue, not overall.
